                  2026 UNESCO Heritage Documentation
                </h2>
              </div>

              <div class="text-slate-700 text-[15.5px] leading-relaxed space-y-4">
                <p>
                  Official documentation provides important historical and administrative context:
                </p>
                <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-slate-600 text-sm">
                  <li>On <strong>July 15, 2026</strong>, Viet Nam submitted the Cu Chi Tunnels to UNESCO's World Heritage Tentative List under cultural criteria <strong>(iv)</strong> and <strong>(vi)</strong>.</li>
                  <li>A Tentative List submission is an inventory step required before a formal nomination for inscription can be examined by the World Heritage Committee.</li>
                  <li>UNESCO identifies Ben Duoc and Ben Dinh as the two areas that best retain the original appearance, scale and structure of the tunnel system, with a combined preserved length of approximately 4 km.</li>
                  <li>Official heritage figures record that the Cu Chi complex welcomed over <strong>27 million cumulative visitors</strong> by the end of 2025.</li>
                  <li>Foreign visitor admission is <strong>135,000 VND</strong> at the time of publication (consisting of 35,000 VND relic entrance fee and 100,000 VND site guide service fee).</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
